          "text": "Now I understand the structure. t1 migrated fully; old cases for t2-t4 remain in cases.cmake and expect files exist.\n\nI need to plan for:\n1. Create expected_t2/t3/t4.hpp generated from tests/expect files (byte-preserving CP1251).\n2. Create t2_cases.hpp, t3_cases.hpp, t4_cases.hpp mirroring cases.cmake.\n3. Handle t4 args: RAD_TOO_MUCH (2 3) as two args, RAD_INNER_SPACE (\"2 3\") as one arg with space, quotes etc. This is important: in e2e Case args is vector<string>, so vector{\"2\",\"3\"} vs vector{\"2 3\"} are distinct naturally — exactly the semantics we want on both platforms now (boost::process builds cmdline with quoting). Need to verify: bp quoting on Windows handles spaces and quotes and backslashes correctly. Probe showed args preserved for \"2 3\", \"2\\\" 3\", \"10\\\\\", \"++10\", \"\" — yes! The probe tested exactly these. Good.\n4. io-fail cases: only on UNIX. In e2e we need compile-time or runtime conditional. Options: `#ifdef __unix__` in cases or exclude via CMake. Also run_with_stdin with a directory FILE*.\n   - Under Windows, opening a directory fails, so skip. Need to think: t3/t4 io-fail tests use run_with_stdin with fopen(FOLDER_INPUT, \"rb\") — on Linux that works; on Windows fopen on dir fails → cannot run. Plan: guard with `#ifndef _WIN32` and/or a separate case list. But a test target compiled on both platforms: use `#if defined(__unix__) || defined(__APPLE__)`. Actually can just do `#ifndef _WIN32`.\n   - Need directory open with fopen and pass FILE* to run_with_stdin. Probe did it. Need to define E2E_IO_INPUT_DIR compile definition = tests/input_data.\n5. The help expected files: t2_help, t3_help, t4_help; version generated from version_expected.h; arg unknown from expected_common.hpp.\n6. Tests count: t2 22 cases per language, t3 ~32 per lang + io, t4 ~40 per lang. Plus help/version/unknown.\n7. Case naming: t1 has special name prefix stripping. Generalize into a helper `test_name(prefix, kind, case_name)`? t1's convention: case names begin with \"t1_\" then test name becomes \"t1_c_norm\". For t2, we could name cases \"t2_norm\" etc. Could create shared helper in e2e_case.hpp: `std::string test_name(std::string_view target, ExeKind kind, std::string_view case_name)`. Careful with \"DRY: повторила дважды — выноси\" rule: t2-t4 would have same logic as t1, so extract into a common helper in e2e_case.hpp and a common param-name generator. Then t1_e2e.cpp refactor to use it. That's a good architecture decision to propose.\n\nActually maybe better: a shared `e2e.cpp`/`e2e_suite.hpp`? The test body is identical across t1-t4 except the exe paths. Could create a shared header with a function template that takes exe paths? gtest TEST_P must be in each file; but test body could be a common function `ExpectCaseMatches(exe, c)`. That's a duplication of 4 copies otherwise. Options:\n  A. Keep 4 nearly identical `t*_e2e.cpp` (only exe definitions differ). ~30 lines duplicated x4.\n  B. Shared header `e2e_test_body.hpp` with `inline std::string test_name(...)` and `inline void expect_matches(const std::string& exe, const Case& c)`. Each t*_e2e.cpp defines suite + TEST_P one-liner calling it. This satisfies \"повторила дважды — выноси\".\n  I'd recommend B, but note the risk of over-abstracting. It's real duplication of logic (normalize, compare both channels, run), so extraction is justified. Also param name generator can be shared: `param_name(prefix, info)`.\n\nWait — gtest names must be unique across suites? Different tests targets, so fine.\n\n8. Expected header generation: t1's expected_t1.hpp was generated presumably by a script (PowerShell) from tests/expect files. We need to regenerate for t2-t4. Since files are CP1251, generation must read bytes as CP1251 and write CP1251. Important subtlety: raw strings R\"E2E(...)E2E\" will contain `)` sequences? unlikely. But help text may include `)\"`? Raw delimiter E2E avoids accidental termination. Check expected files for \"E2E\" substring? unlikely.\n   Also multi-line raw literals in source: if a raw string contains a line starting in column 0, clang-format may... it won't reformat raw string contents. But the source line with `R\"E2E(...)` - backslash line continuation? Fine.\n   Need also check: expected file content may end with LF; t1 generation stripped trailing LFs? t1_help expected file... normalize strips trailing \\n in comparison, so raw content can include trailing newline; but t1 headers seem without trailing newline. Generate by trimming trailing newlines and CR to keep it clean. Actually normalize() removes CR and trailing LF from both sides, so either way works. But keep same approach as expected_t1.\n\n   Also, `kEmptyInput = \"t1_nul\"` naming — for t2-t4 empty stdin they still use t1_nul. Could rename to a neutral `kEmptyInputFile` eventually; but minimal churn: keep. Hmm, \"t1_nul\" is an input file name shared. It's fine but semantically odd. Plan option: name a new shared file `empty_input`; but that adds duplication. Keep as is? Could rename variable to `kEmptyInput` already there.\n\n9. CMake for t2-t4 e2e: add executables, defines E2E_T2_C etc., E2E_INPUT_DIR, E2E_RESULTS_DIR, E2E_IO_INPUT_DIR; deploy dlls; add_dependencies; gtest_discover_tests with prefixes e2e/t2_e2e/ etc.\n\n10. Remove t2-t4 tests from cases.cmake and their expect files? Careful: Some expect files are shared across t1/t3/t4? t1 uses its own. t2_* only t2. t3_* only t3. t4_* only t4. Shared ones: t3_t4_io_fail, t3_t4_plus_minus_coord, t3_t4_plus_minus_extra, t_arg_unknown, t_arg_unknown_multi, t1_nul (input), t1? t1_help only t1. So after migrating t2-t4, can delete their expect files, but must keep shared t3_t4_*? These would be embedded in expected_t3/t4; after migration they can be deleted too if no old tests remain. But deletion of expect files should happen after tests pass, and per AGENTS policy \"эталоны генерируются на лету, в репо не хранятся\" — actually all expects are in repo. The old system compared files. After migration, expects become embedded headers. Do we keep tests/expect? README says... Let's check README and CHANGELOG for references to tests/expect. Also unit tests use tests/expect? Unit tests embed data. e2e embeds. So eventually tests/expect dir could be removed entirely. But input_data stays (files). Careful: t2_help expected file etc. After migration no check uses expect → tests/expect becomes dead. Plan to delete entire tests/expect after verifying; update README/AGENTS? AGENTS not to edit? We shouldn't modify AGENTS (student's journal) unless asked. README/CHANGELOG yes.\n\nBut the user is in plan mode and wants a plan. Let me check README sections referencing tests, e2e, cases.cmake, and CHANGELOG unreleased. Also root CMakeLists for e2e and format.\n\nLet me grep for cases.cmake/tests/expect references.",
