nd string literals produce CP1251 bytes. The expected text is compared byte-wise against program output (CP1251). Correct. But if a source file is compiled as UTF-8, bytes would differ → tests fail. This is a real trap: `.editorconfig` may not enforce CP1251 (charset unset for cpp). The existing t1 header works because it's CP1251. Must preserve. Add verification step: after generation, run e2e; failing Cyrillic expectations would reveal. Also can check bytes of expected headers (`NonAscii` count > 0).\n\n3. **MSVC `/utf-8` not set** — good; source encoding is ANSI CP1251 (active code page). On the VM, sources are also CP1251, but GCC assumes UTF-8 by default! Wait — the project has been compiling CP1251 sources on Linux with GCC all along. GCC treats source as UTF-8 by default, and invalid UTF-8 bytes in string literals... GCC generally passes bytes through? Actually GCC warns/errors on invalid UTF-8 in some contexts? Historically g++ with CP1251 string literals compiles and emits the same bytes (it doesn't validate unless -finput-charset). Existing tests on Linux pass with CP1251 expectations, so it works. Fine.\n\n4. **Directory input on Linux**: `fopen(dir, \"rb\")` success? Probe confirmed after manual open. Need to add to e2e_runner a helper maybe `open_dir`? Actually run_with_stdin takes FILE*; the test opens it. Where? In `t3_cases` we can't open at static init (leak, ordering). Better in the test body: if case has a flag `stdin_is_dir`, then open the dir in test body and pass. Or simpler: add a separate test `TEST(T3E2E, IoFail)` not parameterized, `#ifndef _WIN32`. That's clear:\n   ```cpp\n   #ifndef _WIN32\n   TEST(T3E2E, IoFail) {\n     auto in = e2e::open_input_dir(E2E_IO_INPUT_DIR); // or fopen directly\n     ASSERT_TRUE(in);\n     auto r = e2e::run_with_stdin(in.get(), exe, {}, out, err);\n     ...\n   }\n   #endif\n   ```\n   But then C and C++ both → two tests. Could parameterize only over ExeKind. Or include an `input_is_dir` bool in Case, and open dir inside the body. Which is cleaner? Adding `bool input_is_dir = false` to Case affects t1 too but default. Then in the body:\n   ```cpp\n   if (c.input_is_dir) { open dir; run_with_stdin(...) } else run(...)\n   ```\n   That keeps one matrix and mirrors the old case list. On Windows we simply don't add these cases (`#ifndef _WIN32`), so the flag path isn't compiled there (but still compiled code). Hmm, `run_with_stdin` is always compiled; fine.\n   Also `Case` is used by t1; adding a field at the end with default initializer makes existing aggregate initializations still valid? Aggregate init with fewer initializers → remaining members value-initialized from default member initializer. Yes, in C++14+ default member initializers apply. So `{\"t1_empty\", {}, \"t1_nul\", exit, Stream::err, kT1Nul}` still works with an added trailing `bool from_stdin = false`. But wait: adding a default member initializer makes `Case` still an aggregate (C++14+). Yes.\n   Actually maybe name `input_is_folder`.\n   For the expected code/primary: 74/err, expected kIoFail. Input file is unused; set `c.input` to \"\" and use E2E_IO_INPUT_DIR when flag set. Or set input to absolute? `Case.input` is string_view; absolute path must be compile-time → E2E_IO_INPUT_DIR macro is a literal, so `E2E_IO_INPUT_DIR` could be used directly in cases header if it includes the define. Simpler: in body, use `c.input_is_dir ? E2E_IO_INPUT_DIR : format(\"{}/{}\", E2E_INPUT_DIR, c.input)`. And open dir vs file separately. Fine.\n   Alternative: keep `run()` for files, and add `run_with_stdin_path`? No.\n\n5. **Test result file paths**: result dir exists. For io tests, result names distinct.\n\n6. **`t4` args with empty string**: In C++ vector<string> `{\"\"}`. Boost passes it. Old CMake `EMPTY_ARG \"\"` result: old tests passed with empty arg. Probe confirms. Good.\n\n7. **Argument \"2 3\" (one string)**: On Windows boost will quote it, program sees one arg \"2 3\" → \"Радиус должен быть числом. Получено