build dir ignored. Fine.\n\n12. **Deleting run_case/check**: In tests/CMakeLists, remove add_executable, add_case, include(cases.cmake), file(MAKE_DIRECTORY). Keep `deploy_vcpkg_dlls`, unit/e2e subdirs, find_package. Also README structure. Also delete tests/run_case.cpp, tests/check.cpp, tests/cases.cmake. Also old `tests/results` in build is stale but build dir ignored. Also `.gitignore` has no results entry — old results written to build only. Fine.\n    **Does format-check include tests/check.cpp/run_case.cpp?** It globs source files; deleting them is fine.\n\n13. **CHANGELOG**: add \"t2-t4 e2e migrated...\"; \"Removed run_case/check/cases.cmake/tests/expect\". Note the versioning/help/knowledge.\n\n14. **README**:\n    - Structure: replace run_case/check description with e2e runner; remove tests/expect; add tests/e2e files (runner, cases, expected headers); mention presets `e2e`/`e2e-release`.\n    - Tests section: explain matrix, expectations embedded, byte comparison CP1251, platform io-fail.\n    - Known limitations: update `--contains` item (check removed) → delete; update Windows ASCII paths item (still CreateProcessA via boost::process v1) — [ФАКТ, ВЫСОКИЙ] with evidence; add v1/v2 note in limitations/README as TODO from journal (\"v1 может перейти в v2; v1 выбран ввиду текста курса\").\n    - Versioning/help references still valid.\n    - Remove \"tests/expect - ожидаемые выводы...\" and adjust e2e presets docs.\n    - Possibly document that e2e data is embedded and how to regenerate (script).\n\n15. **AGENTS journal**: propose adding 2026-10-20 entry summarizing migration of t1? Today's date is 2026-10-20 already has entry for release run + runner tests. The user may want to append t2-t4 after doing. Plan can include optional journal entry + dialog export. Since plan is for the student, include as final step (they manage). But I must not edit AGENTS without request. Ask.\n\n16. **Generator script location/format**: Since we're in plan mode, ask user preference:\n    a) one-off PowerShell snippet (like t1) documented in README;\n    b) commit `tests/e2e/gen_expected.ps1`;\n    c) C++ generator target.\n    Recommend (a) or (b)? Hmm. AGENTS says scripting should be minimal and one entry point. A generator is maintenance tooling; committing it makes regeneration possible after expect deletion... but if expect deleted, generator has no input. Unless generator takes expect dir as arg and expect stays in repo until deletion. Contradiction.\n    \n    Wait, reconsider: should tests/expect be deleted? Policy §4.9 p.3: \"Эталоны генерятся на лету, в репо не хранятся. В репозитории — только исходники (генератор, программа, референс)\". t1 migration deleted t1 expect files, so the project already moved to embedding. Keeping t2-t4 expect after migration would violate \"одна точка истины\" and policy; but deleting loses the ability to regenerate headers. Yet headers ARE the source now (committed). That's consistent with t1. So delete. Generator would be a one-off. If we keep a script that reads git history... no. I'd suggest not committing a generator; document in README that expectations are embedded and where they came from? But then if a help text changes, you must edit the header by hand (CP1251) — that's acceptable but error-prone. Hmm.\n    \n    Alternative: **generate expected headers as part of CMake at configure time from tests/expect**, keeping expect files as the single source of truth. Then no manual header editing, no CP1251 manual writing at all, and expectation updates are edits to expect files. This is arguably better engineering: data lives in one place, code generated. But t1 already has committed generated headers; we'd unify t1 too. That's a bigger architectural change (and CMake script to escape CP1251 → raw strings). CMake can read files in any encoding as bytes (file(READ ... HEX)?). Writing C++ with CP1251 from CMake: `file(READ ...)` treats as string; CMake strings are byte-transparent; `file(WRITE)` writes bytes; but escaping `)E2E\"` and ensuring no transformation is doable. However building raw strings with CMake is awkward but feasible. Yet user's established direction is generated-and-committed headers (t1). Changing now contradicts \"не перепрыгивай\" and the journal's plan. So stick with committed headers, generated once by a documented PS snippet. I'll note the tradeoff and ask.\n    \n    Actually for the plan, I can propose: add a small, documented generator script `tests/e2e/generate_expected.ps1` that takes `tests/expect` → headers. But if expect dir is deleted... it can read from a path argument (e.g., `git show HEAD:tests/expect`?) Too complex. Decision point for user.\n\n    Hmm — maybe don't delete tests/expect at all. The t1 deletion happened, but maybe keeping t2-t4 expects is not harmful? It creates two copies (header + file), a maintenance hazard: changing the file won't affect tests; that's a real trap. Better delete.\n\n    Practical compromise: generate headers once, commit; do not commit a generator; document in README that expectations are embedded and edits go to `expected_*.hpp` (in CP1251). This matches t1's existing precedent: there is no generator in repo for expected_t1.hpp. Consistency wins. I'll recommend this and note the risk.\n\n17. **Running t2-t4 in one go vs incremental**: Plan steps should migrate one task at a time, run `ctest --preset e2e -R t2_` after each, then remove old cases after all pass. Good discipline.\n\n18. **Negative controls** (required by course): \n    - For each task, corrupt one expectation embedded (e.g., change byte in expected header or case code/stream) and verify the exact test fails; revert.\n    - Channel pollution test: add stray fprintf(stderr) in t2/t3/t4 and check `*_stderr_empty`/`*_stdout_empty` analog (e2e's EXPECT_TRUE other.empty() integrated into same test).\n    - Wrong exit code test.\n    - Windows+Linux runs.\n    - Verify io-fail only on Linux and skipped on Windows.\n    - Format check, full debug/release, clean clone.\n\n19. **`e2e_runner` open_file on Windows uses fopen_s** — fine.\n\n20. **Thread safety / parallel ctest**: each case writes unique results files. `e2e_runner_test` writes a.out/a.err fixed names — if run in parallel with itself? only 3 tests, unique suites; MissingOutputDir/MissingErrorDir write to same `a.out`? MissingInput doesn't create. MissingOutputDir opens out in no_such_dir and fails before err; MissingErrorDir opens out `a.out` successfully then err fails → writes/truncates a.out. If the 3 run in parallel (ctest -j), MissingOutputDir doesn't touch a.out; MissingErrorDir touches a.out; MissingInput no. So okay. Not our concern now.\n\n21. **CMake function `add_e2e_test`**: define in e2e/CMakeLists:\n    ```cmake\n    function(add_e2e_target target src exe_c exe_cpp)\n      add_executable(${target} ${src})\n      target_link_libraries(${target} PRIVATE e2e_runner GTest::gtest_main)\n      target_include_directories(${target} PRIVATE \"${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR}\" \"${CMAKE_CURRENT_BINARY_DIR}\")\n      target_compile_definitions(${target} PRIVATE\n        E2E_${...}_C=\"$<TARGET_FILE:${exe_c}>\" ...\n        E2E_INPUT_DIR=... E2E_IO_INPUT_DIR=... E2E_RESULTS_DIR=...)\n      configure_target(${target} OFF)\n      deploy_vcpkg_dlls(${target})\n      add_dependencies(${target} ${exe_c} ${exe_cpp})\n      gtest_discover_tests(${target} TEST_PREFIX \"e2e/${target}/\" NO_PRETTY_VALUES)\n    endfunction()\n    ```\n    But the exe macro names differ (E2E_T1_C etc.) and can't be generated easily in CMake. Could standardize macro names: `E2E_EXE_C`, `E2E_EXE_CPP` for every target, since each target has its own compilation. Then t*_e2e.cpp uses the same macro names → shared test helper can take `E2E_EXE_C`/`E2E_EXE_CPP`. This is cleaner:\n    ```cmake\n    target_compile_definitions(${target} PRIVATE\n      E2E_EXE_C=\"$<TARGET_FILE:${exe_c}>\"\n      E2E_EXE_CPP=\"$<TARGET_FILE:${exe_cpp}>\"\n      E2E_INPUT_DIR=\"${CMAKE_SOURCE_DIR}/tests/input_data\"\n      E2E_IO_INPUT_DIR=\"${CMAKE_SOURCE_DIR}/tests/input_data\"\n      E2E_RESULTS_DIR=\"${CMAKE_CURRENT_BINARY_DIR}/results\")\n    ```\n    Then t1_e2e.cpp refactor to use E2E_EXE_C/E2E_EXE_CPP. Good simplification. And common `e2e_test.hpp` provides:\n    ```cpp\n    inline const std::string& exe_for(ExeKind k) { return k==ExeKind::c ? E2E_EXE_C : E2E_EXE_CPP; }\n    ```\n    But macros defined per target; header included in each target sees them. Fine.\n    \n    Function parameter naming: `add_e2e_test(target c_target cpp_target)`.\n\n22. **Shared test logic extraction**: `e2e_test.hpp`:\n    ```cpp\n    namespace e2e {\n    inline std::string test_name(std::string_view prefix, ExeKind kind, std::string_view case_name);\n    template <class ParamInfo>\n    std::string param_name(std::string_view prefix, const ParamInfo& info);\n    void expect_case_matches(const Case& c);  // uses E2E_EXE_C/CPP etc.? needs kind\n    }\n    ```\n    Hmm, test body needs kind: `expect_case_matches(kind, c)`. For io, needs kind too. Implementation uses macros E2E_*; putting implementation in a header with macros is fine (inline functions). Or pass paths explicitly. Using macros in a header is okay but less explicit. Better: pass `exe`, `input_dir`, `results_dir`? Then each TEST_P passes E2E_* macros. Slightly more code at call site, clearer. Or `e2e_test.hpp` can define:\n    ```cpp\n    inline void expect_case_matches(ExeKind kind, const Case& c) { ... uses macros ... }\n    ```\n    Macros are target-scoped, so fine. I'll recommend explicit function with macros for concision, documented.\n    \n    But careful: the helper is not a test body; using ASSERT_* requires void return (it is void). It must `return` after failure; caller returns naturally. OK.\n    \n    io helper: `expect_case_matches` handles `c.input_is_dir` with `#ifndef _WIN32`? If cases exclude io on Windows, the branch is never taken; but `fopen` on dir code would still compile. Could implement:\n    ```cpp\n    inline std::FILE* open_input_dir(...) ...\n    ```\n    For portability, guard with `#ifndef _WIN32`; on Windows produce `FAIL()` if used.\n\n23. **Test naming per target**: `test_name(\"t2\", kind, name)`. For case \"t2_empty\" → \"t2_c_empty\"; for shared \"version\" → \"t2_c_version\". Same as t1. Good.\n\n24. **Do we need `t2_cases.hpp` to use `make_t2_cases()` with insertions**? Same pattern as t1.\n\n25. **Expected headers naming**:\n    - `expected_t2.hpp`: constants kT2EofName etc.\n    - `expected_t3.hpp`: kT3Test1, ...\n    - `expected_points.hpp` (shared t3/t4 parse + io): kPointCommaCoord, kPointExtraNan, ..., kIoFail, kPlusMinusCoord, kPlusMinusExtra.\n    - `expected_t4.hpp`: radius and outputs/help.\n    But wait, t4 symbol/too_few/too_much/empty/hex/etc also shared with t3 (same content). Put common parse errors in expected_points. t4 has `t4_symbol_coords` which is \"Строка 1. Нечисловые данные: 1 2 x\"? t3 has t3_not_digit_case with different content. Check: t4_symbol_coords 36 bytes vs t3_not_digit_case 36 bytes but different hash likely because t3 has \"1 2 k\"? Not shared.\n    So shared: comma, extra_nan, hex_coord, hex_coord_upper, hex_extra_coord, infinity_coord, inf_coord, nan_payload_coord, overflow_coord, too_few, too_much, underflow_coord, x_ky_z, x_yk_z, x_y_z_4k, x_y_z_k4, plus_minus_coord, plus_minus_extra, io_fail. 19 constants. That's a lot of shared data → good to extract.\n    Note: t3 `too_few_args_case` and t4 `too_few_coords` same; names in cases can use shared constant.\n    Are any of these also in t1? t1's errors are integer parse, different text. No.\n    Could put them all in `expected_common_points.hpp`. Name it `expected_points.hpp`. Good.\n\n26. **Naming of constants**: existing t1 uses kT1Nul, kT1Abc. For t2: kT2EofName, kT2EmptyName etc. For shared: kPointCommaCoord or kPtsCommaCoord. I'd use `kPoint*`.\n\n27. **Count of expected files used**: t1_null input used for version/help/unknown. Fine